- *Result:* **≈70 nJ per point** (20–126), calibrated to Sony's 1.19 W development chip (§C5a).
- *Event count:* background count rate × listening time × live fraction + signal counts, $r_{\rm bg}\,t_{\rm listen}\,(1-f_{\rm dead})+n_{\rm req}=3.66\times10^{8}\ {\rm s^{-1}}\times20.8\ \mu{\rm s}\times0.784+25\approx5{,}990$ events per point in bright sun. The inputs are:
  - the background count rate $r_{\rm bg}=3.66\times10^{8}$ s⁻¹ per macro-pixel, half the fully sunlit 940 nm case of §C6(d) (Eq. (81)) evaluated at the model's PDE of 0.37, i.e. a sunlit scene of reflectance ≈0.45 or a pixel half filled by bright surfaces (§C0 subsection 10.3);
  - a listening time $t_{\rm listen}=20.8$ µs per point, a model input equal to one column period of a 520-line sensor at 25 Mpts/s ($520/(25\times10^{6}\ {\rm s^{-1}})=20.8$ µs);
  - the live fraction 0.784 that the paralyzable form of Eq. (82) gives at the per-SPAD rate $r=r_{\rm bg}/9=4.06\times10^{7}$ s⁻¹ (nine SPADs per 3×3 macro-pixel; subsection 2(a)): $e^{-r\tau_d}=e^{-0.244}=0.784$, so each SPAD is blind 21.6% of the time;
  - $n_{\rm req}=25$ signal counts.
- *Cross-check:* the same rate gives $b=r_{\rm bg}\,\Delta t_{\rm bin}\,N_{\rm shots}\,(1-f_{\rm dead})=3.66\times10^{8}\ {\rm s^{-1}}\times1\ {\rm ns}\times10\times0.784=2.87$ background counts per 1 ns bin, summed over the $N_{\rm shots}=10$ shots of a point, the value §C0 subsection 10.3 uses.
- *Energy:* at 5 pJ per event plus a fixed 40 nJ per point, $E=40\ {\rm nJ}+5{,}990\times5\ {\rm pJ}=70$ nJ.
- *Calibration:* the 70 nJ total sits inside the 40–80 nJ per point that a 1–2 W IMX479-class chip implies at 25 Mpts/s ($1\ {\rm W}/(25\times10^{6}\ {\rm s^{-1}})=40$ nJ); Sony's 1.19 W gives 48 nJ.
- *Range:* from 20 nJ at night (20 nJ fixed, 4 pJ per event) to $60\ {\rm nJ}+(7.31\times10^{8}\times20.8\ \mu{\rm s}\times0.614+35)\times7\ {\rm pJ}=126$ nJ in the fully sunlit scene (live fraction 0.614).

